# =============================================================================
# GIT ATTRIBUTES — Kryozen Studio
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (read this before touching it)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This project had no .gitattributes at all, which is the most likely root
# cause of "I pushed a file through Termux, GitHub shows it in the repo, but
# the live site won't load it."
#
# Without an explicit `binary` declaration, Git decides for itself whether a
# file is text or binary, and can apply *text normalization* to it — line-
# ending conversion (CRLF <-> LF) and, depending on core.autocrlf/core.safecrlf
# settings in the git client, content rewriting on checkout. That's exactly
# right for .html/.css/.js, and exactly WRONG for an image, font, or video —
# normalization run against binary bytes doesn't produce a "slightly
# different" file, it produces a corrupted one. The file still exists in the
# repo (so `git status`/GitHub's file browser look completely normal), it
# still has the right name and the right byte COUNT in many cases, but the
# bytes themselves no longer decode as a valid image — so the browser's
# <img>/<source> silently fails to render it. That failure mode matches
# "appears to exist but doesn't load" exactly, and it's invisible unless you
# diff the file's bytes, not just its presence.
#
# This is more likely on a Termux workflow specifically because Android's
# git client, working directory, and (if files ever pass through shared
# storage / the SD card, which is FAT/exFAT-formatted) filesystem all differ
# from the desktop-git assumptions this attribute file protects against by
# default.
#
# THE FIX: every binary asset type used by this project is explicitly marked
# `binary` below, which is shorthand for `-text -diff -merge` — Git will
# never run text normalization, line-ending conversion, or a text diff/merge
# against these files, regardless of any core.autocrlf setting on whichever
# machine commits or checks them out. Text source files get an explicit,
# consistent LF policy so the *editable* files behave the same way on every
# contributor's machine too.
# =============================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TEXT SOURCE — normalize to LF on commit, checkout as LF everywhere.
# Prevents a different but related class of bug: mixed line endings inside
# config/site.config.js or a .html/.css/.js file (e.g. after editing on one
# machine and pushing from Termux on another) can cause subtle diff noise,
# and in rare cases has been known to trip up naive text-based tooling.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* text=auto eol=lf

*.html  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BINARY ASSETS — never text-normalized, never diffed/merged as text.
# This is the actual fix for the reported bug. Add any new binary
# extension you start using to this list BEFORE committing the first file
# of that type.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.avif  binary
*.ico   binary

*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary
*.mov   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.wav   binary

*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary

*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
